   "comment": "THE LIGHTING INVARIANT. A surface in a TEST frame is recognized as a held SurfaceIdentity iff (a) the frame's lighting change was DISCOVERED to be in-class (illum_inclass=1 -- so an out-of-class change can never be force-matched) and (b) its cone value RELATIVE TO ITS OWN FRAME'S ANCHOR equals the prototype's value relative to the anchor of the frame the prototype was minted in, on ALL THREE channels (exact integer cross-multiplication). The illuminant cancels identically: this is 'discover the cross-frame map and take what it leaves fixed'. Sets lit_recognized=1 and lit_id (the identity's key). A surface whose reflectance was never minted, or whose frame is out-of-class, leaves lit_recognized at its 0 init -- REFUSED, not force-assigned, which is the floor a nearest-assignment mechanism cannot produce. THE IDENTITY IS BOUND BY THE MATCH, NOT SELECTED IN THE EFFECT: the second match pattern binds `sid` by traversing from the REFERENCE frame's own has_identity hub, so the pairing (surface, identity) is decided by the rule's own matcher and lit_id is read straight off the BOUND node. An earlier version selected the identity inside the effect with Pick/PickUnique(Filter(NodesOfType('SurfaceIdentity'), ...)); PickUnique PANICKED with 3 candidates, proving the filter predicate was not applied in effect position and that Pick would have written a SILENT WRONG ANSWER (VISION_PROTOCOL sec 12.3 -- the ambiguous read made loud, then fixed the way the panic message itself prescribes: traverse from the owning hub instead of selecting globally). Flag-gated, OFF by default (sec 9).",
